l,"observed_delta":null,"detail":"At-sign and hash sigils in a social-media genre, marking embedded mentions and tags.","measured":true},{"kind":"positive_holdout","prompt":"We landed in Cairo after midnight when heat still hung over tarmac and taxi drivers shouted destinations across crowded lanes outside arrivals. Our driver Karim wove past trucks loaded with sugar cane, past sleepy checkpoints, past neon signs flickering above shuttered bakeries, narrating each quarter as though reciting family history. Dawn found us on a rooftop near Khan el-Khalili, drinking sweet tea while pigeons circled minarets and delivery boys pushed carts of flatbread through alleys still damp from night cleaning. Days blurred into markets, museums, river boats, spice stalls, copper workshops, crowded metro cars, long walks along Corniche at sunset, arguments over fares, naps under ceiling fans, evenings spent comparing notes with fellow travelers over lentil soup. Karim insisted we visit his uncle, a retired calligrapher living above a paper shop, who showed us reed pens, mulberry sheets, inks ground from gall nuts, and patiently corrected our clumsy strokes for hours. From there we rode to Giza at noon, when crowds thinned and guards dozed in patches of shade beside limestone blocks, and a camel owner offered rides we politely declined. Sandstorms delayed trains south, so we lingered in coffee houses, learned backgammon from old men, watched football on battered televisions, memorized greetings we immediately mispronounced. A shopkeeper named Layla, whose brother drives night buses to Luxor, invited us for dinner with her extended family, and we accepted despite knowing only a few words. Her courtyard smelled of jasmine and charcoal, children chased chickens between plastic chairs, uncles debated harvest prices, aunts pressed extra portions onto our plates. After dinner her grandfather unrolled maps, tracing routes through oases, monasteries, Roman ruins, Nubian villages, marking wells, shrines, ferry crossings with a shaking hand. We copied names into notebooks already full of train times, phone numbers, sketches of doorways, recipes for okra, lentils, pickled turnips. Weeks later in Aswan we boarded a slow felucca, drifting past granite islands, heron colonies, mud-brick houses painted blue and white, waving at fishermen casting nets at dusk. Our captain sang old river songs while his son steered, and stars emerged above silent black water. Near the marina we met a guide who read aloud the welcome sign مرحبا بكم في القاهرة اليوم and explained each word while children giggled at our accents. He led us through those narrow alleys behind the mosque, past a school whose minaret dominated the skyline, into a courtyard where artisans hammered brass late into the night.","passed":true,"expected_active":true,"activation":1.486049,"expected_delta":null,"observed_delta":null,"detail":"Long travel narrative placing non-Latin text and noun-projecting determiners deep inside a 500-plus-token passage.","measured":true},{"kind":"counterfactual","prompt":"I saw foo whose bar today. -> I saw foo who bar today.","passed":true,"expected_active":null,"activation":null,"expected_delta":0.48,"observed_delta":0.482183,"detail":"","measured":true},{"kind":"counterfactual","prompt":"mix (x) and [y] and {z} ok. -> mix (x) and [y] and }z} ok.","passed":false,"expected_active":null,"activation":null,"expected_delta":0.47,"observed_delta":0.152795,"detail":"","measured":true},{"kind":"counterfactual","prompt":"ABC 사람 DEF. -> ABC 나무 DEF.","passed":true,"expected_active":null,"activation":null,"expected_delta":0.37,"observed_delta":0.366849,"detail":"","measured":true},{"kind":"counterfactual","prompt":"include <world -> include >world","passed":true,"expected_active":null,"activation":null,"expected_delta":0.64,"observed_delta":0.639167,"detail":"","measured":true},{"kind":"counterfactual","prompt":"} whose car is here. -> } whom car is here.","passed":true,"expected_active